      {
        title: "2. VPC Networking, Routing and Controlled Connectivity",
        explanation:
          "A VPC provides the network boundary, but routing determines where packets travel. Each subnet is associated with a route table. A public subnet commonly has a route to an Internet Gateway, while private application subnets do not expose their instances directly to the internet. When private instances need outbound internet access for updates or external APIs, a NAT Gateway can provide translated outbound connectivity. Database subnets should normally have no direct internet route. Security groups control allowed traffic at the resource interface level, while network ACLs provide stateless subnet-level filtering. These controls complement each other and should be designed around explicit application flows rather than broad unrestricted access.",
        responsibilities: [
          "Internet Gateway: enables internet routing for eligible VPC resources.",
          "Route table: selects the next hop for destination traffic.",
          "NAT Gateway: supports outbound IPv4 connectivity from private subnets.",
          "Security group: stateful allow rules for resource traffic.",
          "Network ACL: stateless subnet-level allow and deny rules."
        ],
        implementation: [
          "Associate public subnet route tables with a default route to the Internet Gateway.",
          "Place NAT Gateways in public subnets and route private application outbound traffic through them.",
          "Allow inbound application traffic only from the load balancer security group.",
          "Allow database traffic only from the application security group on the required database port.",
          "Use VPC endpoints where supported services can be reached privately."
        ],
        productionConsiderations: [
          "A NAT Gateway is Availability Zone scoped; plan resilient routing and cost deliberately.",
