          "Private API access fails because DNS or endpoint requirements are incomplete."
        ],
        practicalTask: "Create a network design with two regional subnets, private application instances, a Cloud NAT gateway and restricted firewall rules. Document the route and firewall path for an application connecting to a managed database."
      },
      {
        title: "3. Global Application Entry, Load Balancing and Edge Protection",
        explanation: "Google Cloud offers global and regional load-balancing products with different traffic types and deployment scopes. An external Application Load Balancer can provide a global HTTP or HTTPS entry point using forwarding rules, target proxies, URL maps, backend services and health checks. Cloud DNS publishes records that direct clients to the application entry point. Cloud CDN can cache eligible content close to users, while Cloud Armor provides configured edge security policies such as application-layer filtering and supported denial-of-service protections. The load balancer does not automatically make the application highly available: backend health, capacity, database dependencies, session handling and regional failure strategy must also be designed and tested.",
        responsibilities: [
          "Cloud DNS: publishes and resolves application DNS records.",
          "External Application Load Balancer: receives and routes HTTP or HTTPS traffic.",
          "URL map: selects backends based on configured host and path rules.",
          "Backend service: defines backend targets, balancing and health-check behavior.",
          "Cloud CDN: caches eligible responses to reduce latency and origin load.",
          "Cloud Armor: applies configured edge security policies."
