          "A URL map routes a host or path to the wrong backend.",
          "Certificate or DNS configuration prevents successful HTTPS access.",
          "An overly strict edge security policy blocks legitimate clients.",
          "Cached content remains stale because cache invalidation or response headers are incorrect."
        ],
        practicalTask: "Design a global HTTPS entry point for a web application with separate frontend and API paths. Define backend health checks, Cloud Armor policy goals and a caching strategy that does not expose authenticated user responses."
      },
      {
        title: "4. Compute Platforms: Compute Engine, GKE and Cloud Run",
        explanation: "Google Cloud provides multiple compute models and they should not be treated as interchangeable. Compute Engine provides virtual machines when operating-system control, specialized agents or legacy software compatibility is required. Google Kubernetes Engine provides managed Kubernetes control-plane capabilities and integrates with Google Cloud networking, identity, logging and monitoring. Cloud Run provides a managed container execution platform for supported request-driven and event-driven services without requiring teams to operate Kubernetes nodes. The correct platform depends on workload behavior, runtime requirements, scaling patterns, deployment model, networking, state management and the team's ability to operate the platform. A production design should avoid selecting Kubernetes merely because containers are used.",
        responsibilities: [
          "Compute Engine: virtual machine workloads and operating-system control.",
          "GKE: Kubernetes orchestration for containerized applications.",
          "Cloud Run: managed container execution with request or event-driven scaling.",
          "Managed instance groups: VM fleet management and autoscaling.",
          "Artifact Registry: stores container images and build artifacts.",
          "Workload Identity Federation for GKE: maps Kubernetes identities to Google Cloud IAM identities."
        ],
        implementation: [
          "Choose Compute Engine for workloads that need VM-level control or specific operating-system capabilities.",
          "Use GKE when Kubernetes APIs, scheduling controls or complex multi-service orchestration justify the operational cost.",
          "Use Cloud Run for suitable stateless services and event-driven container workloads.",
          "Store images in Artifact Registry and deploy immutable image digests.",
          "Configure health probes, resource requests, limits and autoscaling according to workload behavior.",
          "Use service identities and least-privilege IAM rather than embedding service account keys."
        ],
        productionConsiderations: [
          "Measure cold starts, concurrency, startup time and request timeouts for serverless workloads.",
          "Plan GKE upgrades, node pools, capacity, Pod disruption budgets and workload identity.",
          "Use multiple zones where supported and required by the selected compute product.",
          "Separate application configuration from container images and use controlled secret delivery.",
          "Set resource limits and autoscaling thresholds based on observed CPU, memory and request metrics."
        ],
        failureModes: [
          "A GKE workload cannot schedule because of insufficient node capacity or incompatible constraints.",
          "A Cloud Run revision fails startup or exceeds its configured request timeout.",
          "A VM instance group repeatedly recreates unhealthy instances.",
          "A container image cannot be pulled because Artifact Registry permissions are missing.",
          "An application depends on local ephemeral storage and loses state during instance replacement."
        ],
        practicalTask: "Compare Compute Engine, GKE and Cloud Run for a REST API, a long-running worker and a legacy application. Select the best compute platform for each and explain the scaling, identity and operational trade-offs."
      },
      {
        title: "5. Managed Data, Storage and Application State",
        explanation: "Google Cloud provides multiple data services with different consistency, scaling and operational models. Cloud SQL is a managed relational database option for supported engines and common application workloads. Spanner is designed for relational workloads requiring horizontal scale and strong consistency across supported configurations. Firestore provides a managed document database for application patterns suited to document data. Cloud Storage provides durable object storage for files, backups, static assets and data-lake workloads. The architecture must define where authoritative application state lives, how transactions work, how data is encrypted, how backups are retained and how recovery is tested. A highly available compute tier cannot compensate for a single fragile or incorrectly configured data dependency.",
        responsibilities: [
          "Cloud SQL: managed relational database service for supported engines.",
          "Spanner: horizontally scalable relational database with strong consistency.",
          "Firestore: managed document database for suitable application models.",
          "Cloud Storage: object storage with configurable access and lifecycle controls.",
          "Secret Manager: stores and controls access to application secrets.",
          "Cloud KMS: manages cryptographic keys for supported encryption workflows."
