      },
      {
        "question": "What are the most important production risks?",
        "answer": "The key risks include Missing routes, incorrect default routes, blackholed targets, route propagation mistakes and asymmetric connectivity. Reduce them through explicit boundaries, least-privilege access, capacity planning, health monitoring, tested recovery procedures and controlled configuration changes. Verify the controls with practical failure tests."
      },
      {
        "question": "How would you troubleshoot a failure in this architecture?",
        "answer": "Start from the reported symptom and identify the affected user or workload path. Trace Workload Sends Packet -> Destination IP Lookup -> Longest Prefix Match -> Target Gateway or Interface -> Next Network Hop. Check recent changes, component health, permissions, routing, dependency responses and telemetry. Change one variable at a time and confirm recovery with an end-to-end test."
      },
      {
        "question": "Give a practical implementation example.",
        "answer": "Trace traffic from a private application subnet to an external API and identify the route-table entry and next-hop target. Implement the design in a non-production environment first, document its dependencies and security controls, test normal and failure paths, and promote it through a reviewed deployment process. Capture metrics and logs so the production team can operate it reliably."
      }
    ],
