        ],
        "title": "Failure Analysis, Troubleshooting and Validation",
        "productionConsiderations": [
          "Maintain an incident runbook with commands and rollback criteria.",
          "Preserve evidence before making disruptive changes.",
          "Track recurring incidents and convert findings into automated safeguards."
        ],
        "explanation": "Important failure patterns include: Assuming gateway attachment alone grants access, missing public addresses, missing routes and overly permissive security rules. Troubleshooting should start with the user-visible symptom and move through the architecture one boundary at a time. Check recent changes, component health, access decisions, routing, dependency status and telemetry. Validate the recovery with a real request or workload test, then document the root cause and preventive action.",
        "failureModes": [
          "Troubleshooting changes multiple variables and hides the original cause.",
          "A superficial health check passes while the real user path remains broken.",
          "The same failure returns because the root cause was not addressed."
        ],
        "responsibilities": [
          "Detect symptoms and determine the affected architecture boundary.",
          "Collect logs, metrics, traces and configuration evidence.",
          "Restore service safely and prevent recurrence."
        ],
        "practicalTask": "Troubleshoot an EC2 instance that has a public IP but cannot be reached from the internet."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "An internet gateway is a horizontally scaled VPC component that enables internet connectivity for resources with public addressing and suitable routing. It does not by itself make every subnet or instance publicly reachable."
