    "interviewQuestions": [
      {
        "question": "What problem does EKS solve?",
        "answer": "Amazon EKS provides a managed Kubernetes control plane while worker capacity runs on managed node groups, self-managed nodes or serverless compute. Production design includes identity, networking, upgrades and observability. It provides a defined way to organize the components and responsibilities involved. In a production environment, the design should be selected according to workload requirements, security boundaries, operational capability and measurable service objectives."
      },
      {
        "question": "Which components should an engineer understand first?",
        "answer": "Start with EKS control plane, node groups, VPC CNI, CoreDNS, kube-proxy, IAM integration, add-ons and load-balancer controller. Understand what each component owns, how components communicate, which identity authorizes the operation and where health or failure information is recorded. Then trace a complete request or data flow through the architecture."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are the most important production risks?",
        "answer": "The key risks include Node readiness failures, CNI IP exhaustion, authentication mismatch, add-on incompatibility and control-plane access problems. Reduce them through explicit boundaries, least-privilege access, capacity planning, health monitoring, tested recovery procedures and controlled configuration changes. Verify the controls with practical failure tests."
      },
      {
        "question": "How would you troubleshoot a failure in this architecture?",
        "answer": "Start from the reported symptom and identify the affected user or workload path. Trace Developer or CI -> EKS API Endpoint -> IAM Authentication -> Kubernetes Scheduling -> Worker Nodes and Pods -> AWS Services. Check recent changes, component health, permissions, routing, dependency responses and telemetry. Change one variable at a time and confirm recovery with an end-to-end test."
      },
      {
        "question": "Give a practical implementation example.",
        "answer": "Design a private EKS cluster with managed node groups, workload identity and multi-AZ application deployment. Implement the design in a non-production environment first, document its dependencies and security controls, test normal and failure paths, and promote it through a reviewed deployment process. Capture metrics and logs so the production team can operate it reliably."
      }
    ],
