          "Document operational ownership, escalation and change procedures.",
          "Validate the design under normal load and expected failure conditions."
        ],
        "explanation": "GitLab CI defines pipelines in .gitlab-ci.yml and executes jobs through registered runners. Its architecture connects source control, stages, artifacts, caches, protected variables and deployment environments. The design flow is Git Push -> GitLab Pipeline -> Stages and Jobs -> GitLab Runner -> Artifacts and Cache -> Environment Deployment. Treat each boundary as an explicit responsibility: identify who owns the component, which interface it exposes, what data or traffic crosses it, and how its health is measured. Keep the architecture aligned with workload requirements instead of adding services without a clear operational reason.",
        "failureModes": [
          "A hidden dependency fails and interrupts downstream components.",
          "An undocumented configuration change creates behavior different from the approved design.",
          "Capacity or dependency limits are reached before monitoring detects the problem."
        ],
        "responsibilities": [
          "Define the responsibility and trust boundary of GitLab CI.",
          "Document component ownership, dependencies and communication paths.",
          "Connect the design to availability, security, performance and cost objectives."
        ],
        "practicalTask": "Design build, test, scan and deploy stages with separate runners and a protected production environment."
      },
      {
        "implementation": [
          "Build and label each component in the diagram.",
          "Define the interfaces and configuration that connect adjacent components.",
          "Test the complete path rather than testing isolated components only."
        ],
        "title": "Core Components and Traffic or Data Flow",
        "productionConsiderations": [
          "Measure latency and errors at important boundaries.",
          "Ensure logs and traces contain identifiers that connect related operations.",
          "Review scaling limits and dependency quotas before production rollout."
        ],
        "explanation": "The primary building blocks are Pipeline YAML, stages, jobs, runners, executors, artifacts, cache, variables, environments and protected branches. Follow a request or data item from its source through every component until it reaches its destination. At each hop, verify the protocol, identity, routing decision, transformation and response path. This end-to-end view helps engineers distinguish a component failure from a connectivity, authorization or application-level problem.",
        "failureModes": [
          "A missing interface or route breaks communication between components.",
          "A dependency returns errors while the primary component remains healthy.",
          "A configuration mismatch causes partial or intermittent traffic failure."
        ],
        "responsibilities": [
          "Map each core component to one specific responsibility.",
          "Identify the control plane and data plane where applicable.",
          "Document request direction, response direction and dependency order."
        ],
        "practicalTask": "Trace one complete production request through GitLab CI, identifying each component, hand-off and observable signal."
      },
      {
        "implementation": [
          "Apply these controls: Protect variables, isolate runners, use trusted images, define artifact retention and restrict production deployment permissions.",
          "Store secrets in an approved secret-management system rather than source files.",
          "Automate configuration checks and alert on security or availability regressions."
        ],
        "title": "Security, Resilience and Operational Controls",
        "productionConsiderations": [
          "Review access and policy changes regularly.",
          "Test recovery and failover procedures with measurable objectives.",
          "Keep security evidence and operational runbooks available to responders."
        ],
        "explanation": "Production controls for this architecture include: Protect variables, isolate runners, use trusted images, define artifact retention and restrict production deployment permissions. Apply controls at the boundary where they are enforceable and observable. Use least privilege for identities, encryption for sensitive communication, explicit network paths, controlled deployment permissions and tested recovery procedures. Security and resilience must be verified continuously rather than assumed from the initial deployment.",
        "failureModes": [
          "Excessive permissions expose resources beyond the intended boundary.",
          "A single dependency or availability zone becomes an unrecognized single point of failure.",
          "Monitoring does not detect a security or resilience control failure."
        ],
        "responsibilities": [
          "Protect identities, secrets, network paths and stored data.",
          "Define health checks, redundancy and recovery behavior.",
          "Ensure administrative actions and configuration changes are auditable."
        ],
        "practicalTask": "Create a security and resilience checklist for a production deployment of GitLab CI."
      },
      {
        "implementation": [
          "Use the flow Git Push -> GitLab Pipeline -> Stages and Jobs -> GitLab Runner -> Artifacts and Cache -> Environment Deployment as the initial diagnostic path.",
          "Compare actual configuration with the approved design and recent deployment changes.",
          "Validate recovery through health checks and end-to-end tests."
        ],
        "title": "Failure Analysis, Troubleshooting and Validation",
        "productionConsiderations": [
          "Maintain an incident runbook with commands and rollback criteria.",
          "Preserve evidence before making disruptive changes.",
          "Track recurring incidents and convert findings into automated safeguards."
        ],
        "explanation": "Important failure patterns include: Runner compromise, cache poisoning, artifact expiry, incorrect job dependencies and secret exposure. Troubleshooting should start with the user-visible symptom and move through the architecture one boundary at a time. Check recent changes, component health, access decisions, routing, dependency status and telemetry. Validate the recovery with a real request or workload test, then document the root cause and preventive action.",
        "failureModes": [
          "Troubleshooting changes multiple variables and hides the original cause.",
          "A superficial health check passes while the real user path remains broken.",
          "The same failure returns because the root cause was not addressed."
        ],
        "responsibilities": [
          "Detect symptoms and determine the affected architecture boundary.",
          "Collect logs, metrics, traces and configuration evidence.",
          "Restore service safely and prevent recurrence."
        ],
        "practicalTask": "Design build, test, scan and deploy stages with separate runners and a protected production environment."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "GitLab CI defines pipelines in .gitlab-ci.yml and executes jobs through registered runners. Its architecture connects source control, stages, artifacts, caches, protected variables and deployment environments."
