  "argo-cd": {
    "architectureDecisions": [
      {
        "guidance": "Use explicit responsibility and trust boundaries. Keep components independently understandable and document every dependency and communication path.",
        "decision": "How should the architecture be divided?"
      },
      {
        "guidance": "Apply least privilege to users and workloads. Prefer short-lived identity credentials, restricted network paths and auditable policy changes.",
        "decision": "How should access be controlled?"
      },
      {
        "guidance": "Define health checks, timeouts, retry limits, recovery steps and ownership. Avoid unbounded retries and untested automatic failover.",
        "decision": "How should failures be handled?"
      },
      {
        "guidance": "Validate service objectives, security controls, capacity, monitoring coverage, backup or recovery behavior and operational runbooks before release.",
        "decision": "How should production readiness be measured?"
      }
    ],
    "interviewQuestions": [
      {
        "question": "What problem does Argo CD solve?",
        "answer": "Argo CD continuously compares the desired Kubernetes state in Git with live cluster resources. It synchronizes approved changes and reports drift, health and deployment status. It provides a defined way to organize the components and responsibilities involved. In a production environment, the design should be selected according to workload requirements, security boundaries, operational capability and measurable service objectives."
      },
      {
        "question": "Which components should an engineer understand first?",
        "answer": "Start with Application resources, repositories, projects, repo server, application controller, sync policies, RBAC and notifications. Understand what each component owns, how components communicate, which identity authorizes the operation and where health or failure information is recorded. Then trace a complete request or data flow through the architecture."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are the most important production risks?",
        "answer": "The key risks include Repository access failure, incorrect sync waves, configuration drift, excessive permissions and pruning of critical resources. Reduce them through explicit boundaries, least-privilege access, capacity planning, health monitoring, tested recovery procedures and controlled configuration changes. Verify the controls with practical failure tests."
      },
      {
        "question": "How would you troubleshoot a failure in this architecture?",
        "answer": "Start from the reported symptom and identify the affected user or workload path. Trace Git Repository -> Desired Manifests -> Argo CD Controller -> Kubernetes API -> Live Workloads -> Drift and Health Status. Check recent changes, component health, permissions, routing, dependency responses and telemetry. Change one variable at a time and confirm recovery with an end-to-end test."
      },
      {
        "question": "Give a practical implementation example.",
        "answer": "Configure an application to deploy from a Git repository and explain safe sync, drift detection and rollback. Implement the design in a non-production environment first, document its dependencies and security controls, test normal and failure paths, and promote it through a reviewed deployment process. Capture metrics and logs so the production team can operate it reliably."
      }
    ],
