    "interviewQuestions": [
      {
        "question": "What problem does Terraform solve?",
        "answer": "Terraform describes infrastructure as code and builds an execution plan from configuration and state. Production workflows require remote state, locking, review, provider control and safe change management. It provides a defined way to organize the components and responsibilities involved. In a production environment, the design should be selected according to workload requirements, security boundaries, operational capability and measurable service objectives."
      },
      {
        "question": "Which components should an engineer understand first?",
        "answer": "Start with Providers, resources, data sources, modules, variables, outputs, state backend, locking and execution plans. Understand what each component owns, how components communicate, which identity authorizes the operation and where health or failure information is recorded. Then trace a complete request or data flow through the architecture."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are the most important production risks?",
        "answer": "The key risks include State corruption, concurrent applies, accidental replacement, provider drift and exposed state secrets. Reduce them through explicit boundaries, least-privilege access, capacity planning, health monitoring, tested recovery procedures and controlled configuration changes. Verify the controls with practical failure tests."
      },
      {
        "question": "How would you troubleshoot a failure in this architecture?",
        "answer": "Start from the reported symptom and identify the affected user or workload path. Trace Version-Controlled HCL -> Provider and Modules -> Plan -> Review and Approval -> Apply -> Cloud Resources and State. Check recent changes, component health, permissions, routing, dependency responses and telemetry. Change one variable at a time and confirm recovery with an end-to-end test."
      },
      {
        "question": "Give a practical implementation example.",
        "answer": "Create a reusable VPC module and explain how a team safely plans and applies changes through a shared remote backend. Implement the design in a non-production environment first, document its dependencies and security controls, test normal and failure paths, and promote it through a reviewed deployment process. Capture metrics and logs so the production team can operate it reliably."
      }
    ],
