    "interviewQuestions": [
      {
        "question": "What problem does Ansible solve?",
        "answer": "Ansible automates host configuration and application operations through inventories, playbooks, modules and connections. Production design emphasizes idempotency, privilege boundaries, secret handling and controlled execution. It provides a defined way to organize the components and responsibilities involved. In a production environment, the design should be selected according to workload requirements, security boundaries, operational capability and measurable service objectives."
      },
      {
        "question": "Which components should an engineer understand first?",
        "answer": "Start with Inventory, groups, playbooks, roles, modules, facts, handlers, Ansible Vault and execution environments. Understand what each component owns, how components communicate, which identity authorizes the operation and where health or failure information is recorded. Then trace a complete request or data flow through the architecture."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are the most important production risks?",
        "answer": "The key risks include Inventory mistakes, privilege escalation errors, non-idempotent tasks, unreachable hosts and accidental broad changes. Reduce them through explicit boundaries, least-privilege access, capacity planning, health monitoring, tested recovery procedures and controlled configuration changes. Verify the controls with practical failure tests."
      },
      {
        "question": "How would you troubleshoot a failure in this architecture?",
