          "Test the complete path rather than testing isolated components only."
        ],
        "title": "Core Components and Traffic or Data Flow",
        "productionConsiderations": [
          "Measure latency and errors at important boundaries.",
          "Ensure logs and traces contain identifiers that connect related operations.",
          "Review scaling limits and dependency quotas before production rollout."
        ],
        "explanation": "The primary building blocks are Inventory, groups, playbooks, roles, modules, facts, handlers, Ansible Vault and execution environments. Follow a request or data item from its source through every component until it reaches its destination. At each hop, verify the protocol, identity, routing decision, transformation and response path. This end-to-end view helps engineers distinguish a component failure from a connectivity, authorization or application-level problem.",
        "failureModes": [
          "A missing interface or route breaks communication between components.",
          "A dependency returns errors while the primary component remains healthy.",
          "A configuration mismatch causes partial or intermittent traffic failure."
        ],
        "responsibilities": [
          "Map each core component to one specific responsibility.",
          "Identify the control plane and data plane where applicable.",
          "Document request direction, response direction and dependency order."
        ],
        "practicalTask": "Trace one complete production request through Ansible, identifying each component, hand-off and observable signal."
      },
      {
        "implementation": [
          "Apply these controls: Use SSH keys, vault secrets, explicit privilege escalation, idempotent modules, staged rollouts and check mode.",
          "Store secrets in an approved secret-management system rather than source files.",
          "Automate configuration checks and alert on security or availability regressions."
        ],
        "title": "Security, Resilience and Operational Controls",
        "productionConsiderations": [
          "Review access and policy changes regularly.",
          "Test recovery and failover procedures with measurable objectives.",
          "Keep security evidence and operational runbooks available to responders."
        ],
        "explanation": "Production controls for this architecture include: Use SSH keys, vault secrets, explicit privilege escalation, idempotent modules, staged rollouts and check mode. Apply controls at the boundary where they are enforceable and observable. Use least privilege for identities, encryption for sensitive communication, explicit network paths, controlled deployment permissions and tested recovery procedures. Security and resilience must be verified continuously rather than assumed from the initial deployment.",
        "failureModes": [
          "Excessive permissions expose resources beyond the intended boundary.",
          "A single dependency or availability zone becomes an unrecognized single point of failure.",
          "Monitoring does not detect a security or resilience control failure."
        ],
        "responsibilities": [
          "Protect identities, secrets, network paths and stored data.",
          "Define health checks, redundancy and recovery behavior.",
          "Ensure administrative actions and configuration changes are auditable."
        ],
        "practicalTask": "Create a security and resilience checklist for a production deployment of Ansible."
      },
      {
        "implementation": [
          "Use the flow Control Node -> Inventory and Variables -> Playbook -> SSH or WinRM -> Managed Hosts -> Handlers and Validation as the initial diagnostic path.",
          "Compare actual configuration with the approved design and recent deployment changes.",
          "Validate recovery through health checks and end-to-end tests."
        ],
        "title": "Failure Analysis, Troubleshooting and Validation",
        "productionConsiderations": [
          "Maintain an incident runbook with commands and rollback criteria.",
          "Preserve evidence before making disruptive changes.",
          "Track recurring incidents and convert findings into automated safeguards."
        ],
        "explanation": "Important failure patterns include: Inventory mistakes, privilege escalation errors, non-idempotent tasks, unreachable hosts and accidental broad changes. Troubleshooting should start with the user-visible symptom and move through the architecture one boundary at a time. Check recent changes, component health, access decisions, routing, dependency status and telemetry. Validate the recovery with a real request or workload test, then document the root cause and preventive action.",
        "failureModes": [
          "Troubleshooting changes multiple variables and hides the original cause.",
          "A superficial health check passes while the real user path remains broken.",
          "The same failure returns because the root cause was not addressed."
        ],
        "responsibilities": [
          "Detect symptoms and determine the affected architecture boundary.",
          "Collect logs, metrics, traces and configuration evidence.",
          "Restore service safely and prevent recurrence."
        ],
        "practicalTask": "Create a role that installs and configures a web service across a controlled host group with validation and rollback steps."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "Ansible automates host configuration and application operations through inventories, playbooks, modules and connections. Production design emphasizes idempotency, privilege boundaries, secret handling and controlled execution."
  },
