      {
        "question": "Which components should an engineer understand first?",
        "answer": "Start with Templates, parameters, mappings, conditions, resources, outputs, nested stacks, change sets and stack policies. Understand what each component owns, how components communicate, which identity authorizes the operation and where health or failure information is recorded. Then trace a complete request or data flow through the architecture."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are the most important production risks?",
        "answer": "The key risks include Unexpected replacement, dependency cycles, failed rollback, resource deletion and unmanaged manual changes. Reduce them through explicit boundaries, least-privilege access, capacity planning, health monitoring, tested recovery procedures and controlled configuration changes. Verify the controls with practical failure tests."
      },
      {
        "question": "How would you troubleshoot a failure in this architecture?",
        "answer": "Start from the reported symptom and identify the affected user or workload path. Trace Template YAML or JSON -> Validate and Package -> Change Set -> Approval -> Stack Operation -> AWS Resources. Check recent changes, component health, permissions, routing, dependency responses and telemetry. Change one variable at a time and confirm recovery with an end-to-end test."
      },
      {
        "question": "Give a practical implementation example.",
        "answer": "Deploy a VPC stack using parameters and explain how a change set reveals replacement or deletion before execution. Implement the design in a non-production environment first, document its dependencies and security controls, test normal and failure paths, and promote it through a reviewed deployment process. Capture metrics and logs so the production team can operate it reliably."
      }
