    "interviewQuestions": [
      {
        "question": "What problem does Three-Tier Architecture solve?",
        "answer": "Three-tier architecture separates the user-facing presentation tier, business application tier and persistent data tier. Network boundaries and scaling policies help each tier evolve independently. It provides a defined way to organize the components and responsibilities involved. In a production environment, the design should be selected according to workload requirements, security boundaries, operational capability and measurable service objectives."
      },
      {
        "question": "Which components should an engineer understand first?",
        "answer": "Start with DNS, load balancer, web servers, application services, database, private networking, cache and backup systems. Understand what each component owns, how components communicate, which identity authorizes the operation and where health or failure information is recorded. Then trace a complete request or data flow through the architecture."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are the most important production risks?",
        "answer": "The key risks include Database bottlenecks, chatty tier communication, shared failure domains and accidental direct access to data services. Reduce them through explicit boundaries, least-privilege access, capacity planning, health monitoring, tested recovery procedures and controlled configuration changes. Verify the controls with practical failure tests."
      },
      {
        "question": "How would you troubleshoot a failure in this architecture?",
        "answer": "Start from the reported symptom and identify the affected user or workload path. Trace Users -> Web or Presentation Tier -> Application Tier -> Data Tier -> Backup and Monitoring. Check recent changes, component health, permissions, routing, dependency responses and telemetry. Change one variable at a time and confirm recovery with an end-to-end test."
      },
      {
        "question": "Give a practical implementation example.",
        "answer": "Design a highly available three-tier application with public web entry, private application instances and isolated database storage. Implement the design in a non-production environment first, document its dependencies and security controls, test normal and failure paths, and promote it through a reviewed deployment process. Capture metrics and logs so the production team can operate it reliably."
      }
