      }
    ],
    "interviewQuestions": [
      {
        "question": "What problem does Event-Driven Architecture solve?",
        "answer": "Event-driven architecture allows producers to publish events that consumers process asynchronously. Reliable systems define schemas, delivery guarantees, ordering, retries and duplicate handling. It provides a defined way to organize the components and responsibilities involved. In a production environment, the design should be selected according to workload requirements, security boundaries, operational capability and measurable service objectives."
      },
      {
        "question": "Which components should an engineer understand first?",
        "answer": "Start with Producers, event schemas, broker, topics, queues, subscriptions, consumer groups, retry policies and dead-letter queues. Understand what each component owns, how components communicate, which identity authorizes the operation and where health or failure information is recorded. Then trace a complete request or data flow through the architecture."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are the most important production risks?",
        "answer": "The key risks include Duplicate delivery, poison messages, schema incompatibility, ordering assumptions and unobserved consumer backlog. Reduce them through explicit boundaries, least-privilege access, capacity planning, health monitoring, tested recovery procedures and controlled configuration changes. Verify the controls with practical failure tests."
      },
      {
        "question": "How would you troubleshoot a failure in this architecture?",
        "answer": "Start from the reported symptom and identify the affected user or workload path. Trace Event Producer -> Event Bus or Broker -> Routing and Filtering -> Consumer Groups -> Dead-Letter Queue -> Monitoring. Check recent changes, component health, permissions, routing, dependency responses and telemetry. Change one variable at a time and confirm recovery with an end-to-end test."
      },
      {
        "question": "Give a practical implementation example.",
        "answer": "Design an order-created event flow that updates inventory and notifications without tightly coupling the producer to consumers. Implement the design in a non-production environment first, document its dependencies and security controls, test normal and failure paths, and promote it through a reviewed deployment process. Capture metrics and logs so the production team can operate it reliably."
      }
    ],
