        "explanation": "Important failure patterns include: Hidden single points of failure, unhealthy failover, shared dependencies, split-brain behavior and untested recovery paths. Troubleshooting should start with the user-visible symptom and move through the architecture one boundary at a time. Check recent changes, component health, access decisions, routing, dependency status and telemetry. Validate the recovery with a real request or workload test, then document the root cause and preventive action.",
        "failureModes": [
          "Troubleshooting changes multiple variables and hides the original cause.",
          "A superficial health check passes while the real user path remains broken.",
          "The same failure returns because the root cause was not addressed."
        ],
        "responsibilities": [
          "Detect symptoms and determine the affected architecture boundary.",
          "Collect logs, metrics, traces and configuration evidence.",
          "Restore service safely and prevent recurrence."
        ],
        "practicalTask": "Review a web platform and identify every component whose failure could make the entire service unavailable."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "High availability reduces service interruption through redundancy, health detection, failover and elimination of single points of failure. Design must connect availability targets to measurable recovery behavior."
