    "interviewQuestions": [
      {
        "question": "What problem does IAM Architecture solve?",
        "answer": "IAM architecture governs identities and their permissions across people, workloads and services. A secure design separates authentication from authorization and limits access to required actions and resources. It provides a defined way to organize the components and responsibilities involved. In a production environment, the design should be selected according to workload requirements, security boundaries, operational capability and measurable service objectives."
      },
      {
        "question": "Which components should an engineer understand first?",
        "answer": "Start with Users, groups, roles, federation, temporary credentials, policies, permission boundaries and audit logs. Understand what each component owns, how components communicate, which identity authorizes the operation and where health or failure information is recorded. Then trace a complete request or data flow through the architecture."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are the most important production risks?",
        "answer": "The key risks include Over-permissioned roles, long-lived keys, confused deputy paths, weak trust policies and privilege escalation. Reduce them through explicit boundaries, least-privilege access, capacity planning, health monitoring, tested recovery procedures and controlled configuration changes. Verify the controls with practical failure tests."
      },
      {
        "question": "How would you troubleshoot a failure in this architecture?",
        "answer": "Start from the reported symptom and identify the affected user or workload path. Trace Human or Workload Identity -> Identity Provider -> Authentication -> Policy Evaluation -> Scoped Resource Access -> Audit Events. Check recent changes, component health, permissions, routing, dependency responses and telemetry. Change one variable at a time and confirm recovery with an end-to-end test."
      },
      {
        "question": "Give a practical implementation example.",
        "answer": "Design role-based access for developers, CI pipelines and production workloads without sharing static credentials. Implement the design in a non-production environment first, document its dependencies and security controls, test normal and failure paths, and promote it through a reviewed deployment process. Capture metrics and logs so the production team can operate it reliably."
      }
    ],
